\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Physical stack and power path for the two integration alternatives. (a) Embedded: the low-side devices, flying capacitors and inductors---$1226$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m of the $1921$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m component stack at the frozen device heights, $1258$ of $1953$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m at the selected ones---are embedded in the $1.30$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm substrate, which they fill together with the $74$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m through-substrate field; that field carries the 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V input rail. The $685$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m high-side device is flip-chipped below the substrate and is outside the modelled domain. (b) Backside: the converter medium occupies a continuous layer below a $0.30$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}mm substrate and the field carries the 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V output rail at the full cell current; the lateral subdivision of that layer into cells, and the board interface beneath it, are not resolved. Arrow width indicates segment current on a logarithmic scale. The two are electrically distinct despite sharing the same converter.}}{4}{figure.2}}
\newlabel{fig:stack}{{2}{4}{Physical stack and power path for the two integration alternatives. (a) Embedded: the low-side devices, flying capacitors and inductors---$1226$\,\textmu m of the $1921$\,\textmu m component stack at the frozen device heights, $1258$ of $1953$\,\textmu m at the selected ones---are embedded in the $1.30$\,mm substrate, which they fill together with the $74$\,\textmu m through-substrate field; that field carries the 48\,V input rail. The $685$\,\textmu m high-side device is flip-chipped below the substrate and is outside the modelled domain. (b) Backside: the converter medium occupies a continuous layer below a $0.30$\,mm substrate and the field carries the 1\,V output rail at the full cell current; the lateral subdivision of that layer into cells, and the board interface beneath it, are not resolved. Arrow width indicates segment current on a logarithmic scale. The two are electrically distinct despite sharing the same converter}{figure.2}{}}
