\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) Converter loss mechanisms evaluated by \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:pcond}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}--\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:pcoss}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} for one cell, showing the dominance of low-side conduction at a duty cycle of $0.0625$. (b) Electronics-scope loss breakdown for the final pair, separating converter losses from package losses. (c) Segment current and the resulting vertical interconnect loss: the same through-substrate field dissipates essentially nothing carrying 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V and $0.991$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}W carrying 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, which is why the interconnect is not common mode across architectures.}}{6}{figure.3}}
\newlabel{fig:loss}{{3}{6}{(a) Converter loss mechanisms evaluated by \eqref {eq:pcond}--\eqref {eq:pcoss} for one cell, showing the dominance of low-side conduction at a duty cycle of $0.0625$. (b) Electronics-scope loss breakdown for the final pair, separating converter losses from package losses. (c) Segment current and the resulting vertical interconnect loss: the same through-substrate field dissipates essentially nothing carrying 48\,V and $0.991$\,W carrying 1\,V, which is why the interconnect is not common mode across architectures}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Physical realizability of the vertical interconnect. (a) Conductor-plane sequence for the switch-node connection: three material layers separate the terminal faces, but the interlayer redistribution layers are capture planes, so one build-up via level is required. (b) Component tier order before and after the repartition; exchanging the low-side switches with the flying capacitors leaves the component set, per-tier areas and stack height unchanged. (c) Worst via aspect ratio per vertical net at a source-bounded microvia pitch: the $606$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m crossing required by the reference order is eliminated and the worst aspect ratio falls from $5.05$ to $0.833$ at the frozen device heights. The reference order's worst net crosses the low-side tier, so with the selected EPC2057 height ($638$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m) the figure becomes $5.32$ to $0.833$; the repartitioned value is unchanged because it crosses the capacitor tier.}}{6}{figure.4}}
\newlabel{fig:realize}{{4}{6}{Physical realizability of the vertical interconnect. (a) Conductor-plane sequence for the switch-node connection: three material layers separate the terminal faces, but the interlayer redistribution layers are capture planes, so one build-up via level is required. (b) Component tier order before and after the repartition; exchanging the low-side switches with the flying capacitors leaves the component set, per-tier areas and stack height unchanged. (c) Worst via aspect ratio per vertical net at a source-bounded microvia pitch: the $606$\,\textmu m crossing required by the reference order is eliminated and the worst aspect ratio falls from $5.05$ to $0.833$ at the frozen device heights. The reference order's worst net crosses the low-side tier, so with the selected EPC2057 height ($638$\,\textmu m) the figure becomes $5.32$ to $0.833$; the repartitioned value is unchanged because it crosses the capacitor tier}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces Comparison on the frozen repartitioned pair. \textbf  {Every value in this table is a frozen Phase-0M-R3C result and is retained as the historical baseline against which the physical hardening is measured; none of it is a current physical result.} The interconnect rows in particular predate the corrected package model: the package redistribution network was subsequently rebuilt with voltage-fed BGA branches, a separately solved return conductor and finite contacts, which showed that equal ball-current sharing --- assumed when these rows were produced --- does not hold. The superseded rows are \emph  {Intra-cell metal}, \emph  {Vertical transitions} and \emph  {Bumps + package RDL}; the corresponding terms are presently carried as \textsc  {open} in the physical loss ledger rather than replaced by new numbers, because their currents are defined only per named scenario. The corner-joint displacement driver $\Delta u_{\mathrm  {c}}$ and the local inelastic response $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm  {in}}$ are separate quantities and are reported separately: $\Delta u_{\mathrm  {c}}$ is the in-plane displacement the global solve imposes on the joint, $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm  {in}}$ the dimensionless per-cycle strain increment the viscoplastic submodel returns for it. Both architectures are geometrically realizable and process-conditional; neither is qualified.}}{7}{table.2}}
\newlabel{tab:final}{{II}{7}{Comparison on the frozen repartitioned pair. \textbf {Every value in this table is a frozen Phase-0M-R3C result and is retained as the historical baseline against which the physical hardening is measured; none of it is a current physical result.} The interconnect rows in particular predate the corrected package model: the package redistribution network was subsequently rebuilt with voltage-fed BGA branches, a separately solved return conductor and finite contacts, which showed that equal ball-current sharing --- assumed when these rows were produced --- does not hold. The superseded rows are \emph {Intra-cell metal}, \emph {Vertical transitions} and \emph {Bumps + package RDL}; the corresponding terms are presently carried as \textsc {open} in the physical loss ledger rather than replaced by new numbers, because their currents are defined only per named scenario. The corner-joint displacement driver $\du $ and the local inelastic response $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm {in}}$ are separate quantities and are reported separately: $\du $ is the in-plane displacement the global solve imposes on the joint, $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm {in}}$ the dimensionless per-cycle strain increment the viscoplastic submodel returns for it. Both architectures are geometrically realizable and process-conditional; neither is qualified}{table.2}{}}
